0
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,440


1
00:00:03,440 --> 00:00:05,340
We now would like to analyze the force

2
00:00:05,340 --> 00:00:10,850
law on an idealized spring, a force law called Hooke's law.

3
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:15,630
Let's begin by drawing an ideal spring connected

4
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:18,150
to an object on a frictionless surface.

5
00:00:18,150 --> 00:00:21,450
So we'll draw our object.

6
00:00:21,450 --> 00:00:25,300
We'll draw our idealized spring.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:28,900
This surface here is going to be frictionless.

8
00:00:28,900 --> 00:00:32,400


9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:34,340
And this is a wall.

10
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:36,090
And we'll draw it like that.

11
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:40,670
And now what we'd like to do is to introduce the force law

12
00:00:40,670 --> 00:00:45,150
for a spring-- a force law which will be called Hooke's law.

13
00:00:45,150 --> 00:00:47,710
So the way we'd like to analyze this

14
00:00:47,710 --> 00:00:50,100
is by drawing two pictures.

15
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:52,930
Our first picture will have the spring

16
00:00:52,930 --> 00:00:54,970
at an equilibrium picture.

17
00:00:54,970 --> 00:00:58,960
So let's draw the wall again.

18
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:03,360
And let's draw the spring and our object.

19
00:01:03,360 --> 00:01:07,300
And this is called the equilibrium picture.

20
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:12,040
So I'll write equilibrium picture.

21
00:01:12,040 --> 00:01:16,470
And now what we'd like to do is draw a dynamic picture

22
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:18,670
at some arbitrary time t.

23
00:01:18,670 --> 00:01:24,760
So here we have a dynamic picture at time t.

24
00:01:24,760 --> 00:01:27,740
And I'll draw it in a second.

25
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:31,560
So we draw the same object.

26
00:01:31,560 --> 00:01:34,480
And now we're going to move our object to some arbitrary

27
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:36,630
position.

28
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:39,945
In this case, the spring has been stretched, the wall

29
00:01:39,945 --> 00:01:41,950
a frictionless surface.

30
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:45,020
And now I'm in position to choose a coordinate system.

31
00:01:45,020 --> 00:01:50,690
So what I'll do is I'll choose my origin at the edge right

32
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:51,510
here.

33
00:01:51,510 --> 00:01:54,070
This is called x equals 0.

34
00:01:54,070 --> 00:01:56,430
I'll show it in the dynamic picture.

35
00:01:56,430 --> 00:01:59,160
And my coordinate function for the object

36
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:04,820
here, I'm going to refer to that as x of t.

37
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,380
Now, notice that this coordinate function is also

38
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:11,420
an indication of how much the spring has been displaced.

39
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:15,160
As far as directions go, we'll have an axis.

40
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:17,340
And this is our plus x direction.

41
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:22,450
So usually we indicate that with the unit vector i hat.

42
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:26,060
And, now, Hooke's law is the statement

43
00:02:26,060 --> 00:02:33,240
that the force on the spring, that the force on the object F

44
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:37,930
is proportional to how much the object has displaced,

45
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:40,040
which represents either the stretching

46
00:02:40,040 --> 00:02:42,770
or the compressing of the spring.

47
00:02:42,770 --> 00:02:48,950
So it's equal to minus kx i hat.

48
00:02:48,950 --> 00:02:51,410
Now, what does this minus sign mean?

49
00:02:51,410 --> 00:02:56,820
Well, this is an example of what we call a restoring force.

50
00:02:56,820 --> 00:02:59,410
And let's look at just a couple of examples.

51
00:02:59,410 --> 00:03:03,440
When x is positive, that means that the object

52
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:06,010
has been pulled out from the equilibrium position.

53
00:03:06,010 --> 00:03:08,780
The spring is undergoing tension.

54
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:09,960
It's being stretched apart.

55
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:13,430
The molecules in the spring that constitute the spring

56
00:03:13,430 --> 00:03:14,930
are being pulled apart.

57
00:03:14,930 --> 00:03:18,270
And there's a restoring force inside the spring.

58
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:20,140
This is an atomic force in nature

59
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:22,650
that's pulling the spring backwards,

60
00:03:22,650 --> 00:03:26,450
hence exerting the restoring force on the object.

61
00:03:26,450 --> 00:03:30,510
So this force is in the minus i hat direction.

62
00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:32,720
It's restoring in that direction.

63
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:36,610
I'll draw the force like that.

64
00:03:36,610 --> 00:03:39,790
Now, suppose we drew another picture

65
00:03:39,790 --> 00:03:45,270
where the object is pushed in, compressing the spring.

66
00:03:45,270 --> 00:03:49,800
So let's draw, once again, a diagram.

67
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:54,360
And let's show the spring under compression

68
00:03:54,360 --> 00:03:57,560
from our equilibrium position.

69
00:03:57,560 --> 00:04:05,610
Now, here we have, again, that x of t, but in this case, x of t

70
00:04:05,610 --> 00:04:07,470
is negative.

71
00:04:07,470 --> 00:04:11,012
And so this is extension.

72
00:04:11,012 --> 00:04:13,610


73
00:04:13,610 --> 00:04:16,106
So we'll call that case a.

74
00:04:16,106 --> 00:04:22,328
In case b, when x is less than 0, it's under compression.

75
00:04:22,328 --> 00:04:25,470


76
00:04:25,470 --> 00:04:29,040
And then we see that with x negative

77
00:04:29,040 --> 00:04:31,866
and the additional minus sign, the force

78
00:04:31,866 --> 00:04:34,530
is in the positive direction.

79
00:04:34,530 --> 00:04:37,570
And so in both instances, the force

80
00:04:37,570 --> 00:04:40,230
is a restoring force back to equilibrium.

81
00:04:40,230 --> 00:04:42,480
So the restoring force, whether you're

82
00:04:42,480 --> 00:04:47,440
under extension or compression is always bringing--

83
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:55,680
is pointing, let's just call it-- the direction of the force

84
00:04:55,680 --> 00:05:02,470
is towards the equilibrium position.

85
00:05:02,470 --> 00:05:08,050
And this example of a restoring force

86
00:05:08,050 --> 00:05:11,950
is also only proportional to x.

87
00:05:11,950 --> 00:05:15,470
And so in that case, we can add the word linear

88
00:05:15,470 --> 00:05:18,280
because it's just to the single power x.

89
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:21,906
And this is an example of a linear restoring force.

90
00:05:21,906 --> 00:05:27,945
And this is a model for an ideal spring.

91
00:05:27,945 --> 00:05:31,630


92
00:05:31,630 --> 00:05:36,135
Now, this constant k is called the spring constant.

93
00:05:36,135 --> 00:05:38,810


94
00:05:38,810 --> 00:05:41,160
And the units of the spring constant,

95
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:44,850
if you divide the units of force by the units of distance,

96
00:05:44,850 --> 00:05:53,180
so we have SI units are Newton over meters

97
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:56,390
and that's the measure of the spring constant.

